<p>Material choices move these numbers dramatically — if you're weighing plywood grades for humid conditions (very relevant to the Kerala look), our <a href="/materials/marine-plywood/">BWP plywood guide</a> and <a href="/compare/calibrated-vs-normal-plywood/">calibrated vs normal plywood comparison</a> will save you from expensive mistakes. And for overall project planning, see our <a href="/planning/interior-timeline/">interior design timeline guide</a>.</p>
<h2>11.5 Mistakes That Turn "Heritage" Into "Theme Park"</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Covering every surface.</strong> If the ceiling, walls, floor, and furniture all shout, nothing is heard. Pick your hero surfaces; mute the rest.</li>
  <li><strong>Fake-ageing everything.</strong> Distressed finishes on brand-new mass-produced pieces read costume-y. One genuinely old or honestly handmade piece beats ten "antiqued" ones.</li>
  <li><strong>Ignoring scale.</strong> A palace-sized jharokha in an 11-foot-wide living room overwhelms. Size statement pieces to the room, not the showroom.</li>
  <li><strong>Wrong lighting temperature.</strong> Cool white (6500K) LEDs murder both styles. Warm white (2700–3000K) is non-negotiable for wood, brass, and jewel tones to glow.</li>
  <li><strong>Skipping the modern counterweight.</strong> Without clean-lined modern pieces to rest the eye, regional elements lose their impact. Contrast is the frame that makes craft visible.</li>
</ol>
